  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 322 AM EDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nEarly this morning, water vapor imagery shows a well-defined \nmidlevel low spinning over the Pacific NW, with more subtle \nshortwave action over the Northern Plains, and the remnants of \nIsaias over western TN. This has helped shift the midlevel ridge \nmore eastward, with the axis directed over the Lower Great Lakes. At \nthe surface, weakening low pressure is moving into northern MN while \nsprawling surface high pressure is centered offshore of New England. \nThis is keeping a fairly tight pressure gradient over the UP, though \nwill note that the gradient is weaker compared to yesterday as the \nsurface low has weakened. Wind gusts are beginning to fall back as a \nresult, though southerly wind gusts still remain breezy with \nwidespread gusts around 20-30mph still being reported across the UP. \nOtherwise, temperatures remain quite warm, hovering in the 60s and \neven near 70 in some of the downsloping areas. \n\nThe surface low continues to weaken and elongate as we head into the \ndaytime hours, with weak low pressure finally passing over the UP \nduring the afternoon and evening. Persistent southerly flow keeps \nour very warm airmass in place, with highs once again ranging well \ninto the 70s to lower 80s for most of the UP (away from Lake \nMichigan). Meanwhile, southerly wind gusts gradually decrease \nthroughout the day, finally falling back below 20mph closer to \nsunset. \n\nTonight into Monday, a cold front slowly drops southward from \nOntario while another shortwave ejects out of the Plains. Clouds \nincrease over the UP, and though some shower activity is possible in \nthe proximity of the front over Lake Superior and the northern UP, \nthis has plenty of dry air to contend with. Therefore, dry weather \npersists for most Monday, while temperatures rise from morning lows \nin the 50s to highs in the 70s away from Lake Superior. Near the \nlake, winds shifting out of the northeast should limit high \ntemperatures to the 60s. The aforementioned shortwave moves through \nMonday night, bringing a quick round of widespread, if light, rain \nshowers to the area. With some elevated instability indicated in \nsoundings, a few rumbles of thunder are not out of the question. \nLatest guidance has a widespread 50-60% chance for rain totals \naround/in excess of a quarter inch by the time rain wraps up Tuesday \nmorning. \n\nRidging recovers later Tuesday into Wednesday, bringing a return to \ndry weather while temperatures trend more seasonal. Towards the \nlatter half of the work week, the general setup among deterministic \nand ensemble guidance is for another midlevel low to close off and \nspin over the Rockies while a deepening trough drops out of Alberta. \nAnd this is where the similarities end, with some of the guidance \nshowing a phasing of these two systems and a deep low pressure \nsystem moving over the UP, while other guidance just brings the more \nnorthern wave through. This all brings the potential for more rounds \nof wet weather into the forecast towards the end of the work week, \nas well as a shot of colder air into the weekend, though confidence \nin the finer points is low.",
